1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,707
A washing machine spins wet clothes.

2
00:00:02,707 --> 00:00:06,144
A car takes a sharp turn,
and you slide on the seat.

3
00:00:06,544 --> 00:00:07,913
Both are going round.

4
00:00:07,913 --> 00:00:10,891
And something does not
want to stay on the circle.

5
00:00:11,341 --> 00:00:13,083
Diya spins a salad spinner.

6
00:00:13,083 --> 00:00:16,531
The leaves stay in the basket,
but the water flies off.

7
00:00:16,881 --> 00:00:20,796
What does the basket do for the leaves,
that it cannot do for the water?

8
00:00:21,296 --> 00:00:25,130
By the end of this video, you will
measure it with one short formula.

9
00:00:25,780 --> 00:00:28,009
So what is centripetal acceleration?

10
00:00:28,359 --> 00:00:32,992
A ball going round at a steady speed
still accelerates, because its velocity

11
00:00:32,992 --> 00:00:34,041
keeps turning.

12
00:00:34,441 --> 00:00:37,309
That acceleration always
points to the centre.

13
00:00:37,309 --> 00:00:39,376
Centripetal means centre seeking.

14
00:00:39,376 --> 00:00:40,777
But how big is it?

15
00:00:41,377 --> 00:00:43,627
Let's run it in our motion notebook.

16
00:00:43,627 --> 00:00:46,026
A ball goes round a two metre circle.

17
00:00:46,376 --> 00:00:50,301
At two metres per second, the
red acceleration arrow is short.

18
00:00:50,301 --> 00:00:52,555
Just two metres per second squared.

19
00:00:52,955 --> 00:00:54,950
Now double the speed to four.

20
00:00:54,950 --> 00:00:56,394
Watch the red arrow.

21
00:00:56,794 --> 00:00:58,174
It jumps to eight.

22
00:00:58,174 --> 00:01:01,221
Double the speed, four
times the acceleration.

23
00:01:01,621 --> 00:01:05,241
On the graph, the dot climbs
a curve, not a straight line.

24
00:01:05,241 --> 00:01:06,752
a grows with v squared.

25
00:01:07,252 --> 00:01:08,602
Let's slow it down.

26
00:01:08,952 --> 00:01:12,937
Without any pull, the ball would carry
straight on along the tangent, like

27
00:01:12,937 --> 00:01:13,837
the water.

28
00:01:14,070 --> 00:01:16,376
The real ball keeps bending inward.

29
00:01:16,376 --> 00:01:20,226
That bending toward the centre
is the centripetal acceleration.

30
00:01:20,876 --> 00:01:22,376
Now, how big is it?

31
00:01:22,376 --> 00:01:23,782
One step at a time.

32
00:01:24,132 --> 00:01:25,032
Step one.

33
00:01:24,977 --> 00:01:29,973
In a short time, delta t, the ball
moves a tiny distance, v delta t.

34
00:01:30,723 --> 00:01:31,623
Step two.

35
00:01:31,531 --> 00:01:34,724
The radius turns by a
small angle, delta theta.

36
00:01:34,724 --> 00:01:39,504
The velocity stays at right angles
to it, so it turns by the same angle.

37
00:01:40,254 --> 00:01:41,333
Step three.

38
00:01:41,333 --> 00:01:44,712
So these two triangles
have the same shape.

39
00:01:44,712 --> 00:01:45,791
Same ratio.

40
00:01:45,791 --> 00:01:49,076
Delta v over v equals v delta t over r.

41
00:01:49,826 --> 00:01:50,848
Step four.

42
00:01:50,848 --> 00:01:52,482
Divide by delta t.

43
00:01:52,482 --> 00:01:57,385
The acceleration, delta v over
delta t, is v squared over r.

44
00:01:58,135 --> 00:01:59,054
Step five.

45
00:01:59,054 --> 00:01:59,954
Units.

46
00:01:59,698 --> 00:02:04,832
Metres per second, squared, over
metres, gives metres per second squared.

47
00:02:04,832 --> 00:02:08,699
And since v equals omega r,
a equals omega squared r.

48
00:02:09,449 --> 00:02:10,559
Remember this.

49
00:02:10,559 --> 00:02:15,310
Centripetal acceleration is v squared
over r, and it always points to the

50
00:02:15,310 --> 00:02:16,210
centre.

51
00:02:16,695 --> 00:02:17,721
Now a puzzle.

52
00:02:17,721 --> 00:02:21,086
Does a bigger circle mean
more acceleration, or less?

53
00:02:21,436 --> 00:02:24,501
Keep the speed the same,
four metres per second.

54
00:02:24,501 --> 00:02:26,607
The two metre circle gives eight.

55
00:02:26,607 --> 00:02:29,002
The four metre circle gives only four.

56
00:02:29,402 --> 00:02:32,950
Now keep the turning rate
the same, on one turning rod.

57
00:02:32,950 --> 00:02:37,074
The outer ball moves faster, and
its acceleration is twice as big.

58
00:02:37,674 --> 00:02:38,691
Now you try.

59
00:02:38,691 --> 00:02:43,739
You stand at the edge of a spinning
merry go round, then walk in to halfway.

60
00:02:43,739 --> 00:02:46,229
Does your acceleration go up or down?

61
00:02:46,229 --> 00:02:47,493
Pause and decide.

62
00:02:50,693 --> 00:02:51,852
Down, to half!

63
00:02:51,852 --> 00:02:55,497
You turn at the same rate,
and a equals omega squared r.

64
00:02:55,497 --> 00:02:58,002
Half the radius, half the acceleration.

65
00:02:58,652 --> 00:03:00,767
So why does the water fly off?

66
00:03:00,767 --> 00:03:02,014
Follow the links.

67
00:03:02,364 --> 00:03:03,264
One.

68
00:03:02,873 --> 00:03:07,023
To go round, every bit needs an
acceleration toward the centre.

69
00:03:07,423 --> 00:03:08,323
Two.

70
00:03:07,883 --> 00:03:09,730
Something must push it inward.

71
00:03:09,730 --> 00:03:12,370
For the leaves, the basket wall does that.

72
00:03:12,770 --> 00:03:13,670
Three.

73
00:03:13,337 --> 00:03:15,728
The water slips out through the holes.

74
00:03:15,728 --> 00:03:17,876
Nothing pushes it inward any more.

75
00:03:18,276 --> 00:03:19,176
Four.

76
00:03:18,846 --> 00:03:21,258
So it flies off along the tangent.

77
00:03:21,258 --> 00:03:23,090
It is not thrown outward.

78
00:03:23,090 --> 00:03:24,882
It is just not held in.

79
00:03:25,482 --> 00:03:26,382
One more.

80
00:03:26,300 --> 00:03:28,816
Diya turns the spinner twice as fast.

81
00:03:28,816 --> 00:03:32,251
How many times more acceleration
does the water need?

82
00:03:32,251 --> 00:03:33,479
Pause and think.

83
00:03:36,679 --> 00:03:37,628
Four times!

84
00:03:37,628 --> 00:03:41,187
a equals omega squared r,
and two squared is four.

85
00:03:41,837 --> 00:03:43,573
Now, one worked example.

86
00:03:43,923 --> 00:03:48,384
The spinner basket has a radius of
ten centimetres, and turns five times

87
00:03:48,384 --> 00:03:49,340
each second.

88
00:03:49,340 --> 00:03:52,285
Find the acceleration
of a leaf at the wall.

89
00:03:52,785 --> 00:03:54,897
Omega is two pi times five.

90
00:03:54,897 --> 00:03:57,451
That is ten pi radians per second.

91
00:03:58,201 --> 00:04:00,128
a equals omega squared r.

92
00:04:00,128 --> 00:04:03,095
Ten pi, squared, times zero point one.

93
00:04:03,845 --> 00:04:08,286
That is ten pi squared, about ninety
eight point seven metres per second

94
00:04:08,286 --> 00:04:09,186
squared.

95
00:04:09,732 --> 00:04:11,533
That is about ten times g.

96
00:04:11,533 --> 00:04:14,004
A strong push, all toward the centre.

97
00:04:14,654 --> 00:04:16,119
Now, the exam trap.

98
00:04:16,469 --> 00:04:20,728
Many students say centripetal
acceleration points outward, because the

99
00:04:20,728 --> 00:04:21,978
water flies out.

100
00:04:22,328 --> 00:04:24,049
But it always points inward.

101
00:04:24,049 --> 00:04:27,260
Loose water leaves along the
tangent, not straight out.

102
00:04:27,910 --> 00:04:29,411
Where is this asked?

103
00:04:29,411 --> 00:04:33,854
As short numericals: a from speed,
radius or period, and ratios.

104
00:04:34,454 --> 00:04:35,701
Exam level now.

105
00:04:35,701 --> 00:04:39,239
A goes round a one metre
circle every two seconds.

106
00:04:39,239 --> 00:04:42,883
B goes round a four metre
circle every four seconds.

107
00:04:42,883 --> 00:04:45,534
Find the ratio of their accelerations.

108
00:04:45,534 --> 00:04:46,833
Pause and solve.

109
00:04:52,233 --> 00:04:53,646
The answer is B.

110
00:04:53,646 --> 00:04:54,720
One to one.

111
00:04:55,220 --> 00:05:01,092
With omega equal to two pi over T, a
equals four pi squared r over T squared.

112
00:05:01,592 --> 00:05:04,543
For A, four pi squared,
times one over four.

113
00:05:04,543 --> 00:05:05,875
That is pi squared.

114
00:05:06,375 --> 00:05:10,021
For B, four pi squared,
times four over sixteen.

115
00:05:10,021 --> 00:05:11,345
Also pi squared.

116
00:05:11,345 --> 00:05:12,399
One to one.

117
00:05:12,999 --> 00:05:14,458
Here is your recap.

118
00:05:14,458 --> 00:05:15,957
Screenshot this card.

119
00:05:19,157 --> 00:05:22,156
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

120
00:05:22,556 --> 00:05:23,456
One.

121
00:05:23,030 --> 00:05:27,345
Why does the water fly off a salad
spinner, while the leaves stay in?

122
00:05:28,245 --> 00:05:29,145
Two.

123
00:05:28,735 --> 00:05:31,296
On the same circle, the speed doubles.

124
00:05:31,296 --> 00:05:33,406
What happens to the acceleration?

125
00:05:34,306 --> 00:05:35,206
Three.

126
00:05:34,987 --> 00:05:38,238
Is centripetal acceleration
a constant vector?

127
00:05:38,238 --> 00:05:39,622
Why or why not?

128
00:05:40,722 --> 00:05:45,420
Next time: rain falls straight down,
but when Aarav cycles, it hits his

129
00:05:45,420 --> 00:05:46,320
face.

130
00:05:45,982 --> 00:05:48,562
Which way should he tilt his umbrella?

131
00:05:48,562 --> 00:05:51,914
We find out in relative
velocity in two dimensions.
